<!--
  009 DDS configuration: pin discovery to loopback, and raise the participant ceiling.

  WHY LOOPBACK

  Without this file, 009 nodes see each other only some of the time. The symptom is
  non-deterministic, and therefore expensive: `ros2 node list` returns /r01/safety_gate
  but not /r01/gz_bridge on one run, and the exact opposite on the next, while `ros2 topic
  echo` on the missing node's topic says "does not appear to be published yet". Nothing has
  crashed. The graph is simply incomplete, and an incomplete graph reads exactly like a
  broken bridge.

  The cause is the network this runs on. WSL2 exposes several interfaces at once:

      lo      127.0.0.1
      eth1    169.254.83.107/16    link local, no peer
      eth3    192.168.31.199/24    the LAN
      eth5    169.254.226.131/16   link local, no peer

  Default multicast discovery sends from whichever interface the routing table prefers.
  Two participants that choose differently never meet, and which pairs meet is a race,
  precisely the observed behaviour. Pinning discovery to `lo` removes the choice.

  WHY THE PARTICIPANT CEILING IS RAISED, AND WHY THAT WAS NOT OPTIONAL

  This file used to say that the participant index was deliberately left at its library
  default. That was a guess, and P3 falsified it. Measured on this machine, with this file
  at the default:

      concurrent node processes created and held : 32
      refused                                    : the 33rd
      error   create: Failed to find a free participant index for domain 42
              rmw_cyclonedds_cpp: rmw_create_node: failed to create domain

  A two-robot 009 stack is about 30 processes: two Nav2 stacks, two gates, two robot
  bridges, the world launch, the clock and truth bridges, and the spawn helpers. That sits
  just under the ceiling, so P3 landed exactly on the limit and failed in an
  order-dependent way. `ros_gz_sim create` for the SECOND robot could not join the domain,
  aborted with SIGABRT, and that robot was never spawned at all. None of the visible
  symptoms pointed at the cause: the log said

      Invalid frame ID "r02/odom" passed to canTransform argument target_frame
      Failed to activate local_costmap because transform from r02/base_link to r02/odom
      Failed to change state for node: controller_server
      Failed to bring up all requested nodes. Aborting bringup.

  which reads like a TF or costmap bug, and cost a P3 run to trace back to DDS.

  So the ceiling is raised, and the element has to go in the right place. This is a
  Discovery setting, not a Domain one, and the library says so plainly once you ask it:

      <Domain><Discovery><MaxAutoParticipantIndex>200</MaxAutoParticipantIndex></Discovery>
      accepted, node starts

      <Domain><Ports><MaxAutoParticipantIndex>200</...></Ports>
      config: //CycloneDDS/Domain: Ports: unknown element
      -> and then EVERY process fails to create a node, so the ceiling becomes zero

  The placement was found by reading the element names out of libddsc.so.11, where
  MaxAutoParticipantIndex appears between Peers, SPDPMulticastAddress and SPDPInterval,
  i.e. in the Discovery group. Worth recording because the obvious guess (a "Ports"
  section, which does exist as a name elsewhere in that table) is silently wrong, and
  silently wrong here means zero participants rather than a warning.

  scripts/_dds_capacity_node.py holds one participant per process and reports how many
  fit, so this number is measured rather than assumed.

  A NOTE ON THIS FILE ITSELF: XML comments may not contain a double hyphen. An earlier
  version of this comment did, the file stopped being well formed XML, CycloneDDS fell
  back to a broken configuration, and the measured ceiling became ZERO participants. That
  is the same mistake this project already made twice in SDF files, which is why
  scripts/check_xml_wellformed.py now parses every XML file in the tree during the build.

  IMPORTANT LIMITATION

  Loopback-only discovery is correct for a single-host simulation and WRONG for a real
  multi-machine fleet, where the robots are separate hosts. When 009 moves beyond one
  machine, this file must gain the real interface, or be replaced by a discovery server.
  It is not a harmless default to leave in place unexamined.
-->
<CycloneDDS xmlns="https://cdds.io/config">
  <Domain id="any">
    <General>
      <Interfaces>
        <NetworkInterface name="lo" />
      </Interfaces>
    </General>
    <Discovery>
      <MaxAutoParticipantIndex>200</MaxAutoParticipantIndex>
    </Discovery>
  </Domain>
</CycloneDDS>
